title: Sumo Logic search queries, hands-on
guide: howto/sumo-logic-queries.html
promise: Learn Sumo Logic's search language from zero on 24 events from CloudTrail, then turn the searches into alerts.

== Who it is for and what you need
section: How to use this guide
It is for beginners new to Sumo Logic, a hosted log analytics and security platform, and to writing searches, who can use a terminal.

You need about two hours, curl and jq, and an organisation that accepts your own data.

== How the guide was checked
section: How to use this guide
It was written without a Sumo Logic account, so no query was run: each result is Sumo Logic's documented behaviour, checked on 27 September 2026, applied to the lab file by a local script.

Step 9's monitor was never applied.

== How to follow the guide
section: How to use this guide
Read it in order; steps 1 to 3 load the data. Run SUMO LOGIC blocks in Log Search, the page where you search your logs; paste BASH blocks into a terminal. "Expect" sentences say what queries should return.

== Set up: a 30-day trial that takes your data
section: 1. Get an organisation you can send data to
The Agentic Free Trial cannot load your data; the Amazon Web Services (AWS) Marketplace trial can, with a business email and Sumo Logic's licence agreement. After 30 days AWS bills usage monthly unless cancelled in AWS Marketplace under Manage subscriptions.

== Set up: load the lab data and count it
section: 3. Load the lab data and check what arrived
Step 2 creates the upload address for SUMO_URL. Step 3's block writes the lab file, then these lines check it, move it to yesterday and upload it. Expect 200. At Log Search's time range -2d, step 3's query counts 24.
```bash
shasum -a 256 cloudtrail-lab.jsonl

jq -c '(now - 86400 | strftime("%Y-%m-%d")) as $d | .eventTime |= ($d + .[10:])' \
  cloudtrail-lab.jsonl > upload.jsonl

SUMO_URL='https://paste-your-presigned-url-here'
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST \
  -H 'X-Sumo-Category:lab/aws/cloudtrail' -T upload.jsonl "$SUMO_URL"
```

== Where you meet Sumo Logic's search language
section: What Sumo Logic's search language is for
Logs are lines of text that each record one event. In Sumo Logic you search them with queries in Log Search.

Analysts write queries to investigate; detection engineers turn the ones worth repeating into monitors, Sumo Logic's alerting feature.

== The lab: 24 CloudTrail events and a leaked key
section: Walk-through
The lab is 24 events from CloudTrail, the record AWS keeps of actions taken in an account: who made each call, from which address, and whether AWS refused it.

A continuous integration user's access key, build-bot, has leaked.

== What someone does with build-bot's key
section: Walk-through
From 203.0.113.50 someone is mostly refused, then creates the user backup-svc, gives it administrator rights (the AdministratorAccess policy) and an access key, and uses it to stop CloudTrail logging.

Ordinary activity includes a console login without multi-factor authentication (MFA).

== include: _cloudtrail-lab.deck

== A field does not exist until a query parses it
section: How a query is put together
Sumo Logic stores each event as raw text. Until something parses a field, it does not exist.

In this query, the scope before the first pipe (|) chooses messages; the json operator creates eventName for the where operator to compare.
```sumo
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "eventName"
| where eventName = "StopLogging"
```

== D1: the parse step filters too
section: 4. Filter on a parsed field: trail tampering (D1)
By default the json operator drops every message that lacks a key it asks for. Roles have no userIdentity.userName, the userName key inside userIdentity, so nodrop keeps their events, with the field empty. Expect one message: StopLogging by backup-svc.
```sumo
// D1: CloudTrail logging stopped, deleted or changed
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "eventName", "sourceIPAddress", "userIdentity.userName" as eventName, sourceIPAddress, userName nodrop
| where eventName in ("StopLogging", "DeleteTrail", "UpdateTrail", "PutEventSelectors")
| fields eventName, userName, sourceIPAddress
```

== Before you trust an empty result, run a control
section: 6. Count, then threshold: the burst that never fires (D2)
The naive D2, counting only AccessDenied, returns nothing: the attacker has 4. This control, a query that must return something, counts codes: AccessDenied 7, four of them the attacker's, and Client.UnauthorizedOperation 3, the attacker's Elastic Compute Cloud (EC2) refusals.
```sumo
// Control: which error codes are in the data at all?
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "errorCode" nodrop
| if(isBlank(errorCode), "(none)", errorCode) as errorCode
| count by errorCode
```

== D3: the 10-minute window is the detection
section: 8. One user, two events, ten minutes (D3)
The transaction operator groups events by newUser, counts each named state and records the first and last times in milliseconds. Expect backup-svc only. Without the last three lines, svc-reporting, routine work keyed 2 hours 37 minutes after creation, appears too.
```sumo
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "eventName", "requestParameters.userName" as eventName, newUser nodrop
| where eventName in ("CreateUser", "CreateAccessKey")
| transaction on newUser
    with states CreateUser as created, CreateAccessKey as keyed in eventName
    results by transactions
| where created > 0 and keyed > 0
| _end_time - _start_time as gap_ms
| where gap_ms <= 10m
| gap_ms / 1000 as gap_seconds
```

== Turn D2 into a monitor
section: 9. Turn the search into a monitor
Step 9 builds it under Monitoring > Monitors > Add > New Monitor, or, as shown here in part, in a Terraform file; Terraform creates resources from text files. The fixed D2 keeps its threshold, so any row alerts.
```hcl
      | where _count >= 5
    EOT
  }
  trigger_conditions {
    logs_static_condition {
      critical {
        time_range = "10m" # look at the last 10 minutes,
        frequency  = "1m"  # every minute
        alert {
          threshold      = 0
          threshold_type = "GreaterThan" # any row at all
```

== Test the monitor both ways
section: 10. Test the monitor both ways
In ~/sumo-lab, with SUMO_URL still set, these commands replay 192.0.2.20's three denials stamped now, because the monitor checks only the last 10 minutes. Five minutes later it must still be Normal. Rerun with --arg ip 203.0.113.50: it should go Critical.
```bash
jq -c --arg ip 192.0.2.20 '(now | strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")) as $t
  | select(.sourceIPAddress == $ip and has("errorCode")) | .eventTime = $t' \
  cloudtrail-lab.jsonl > replay.jsonl
curl -sS -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}\n' -X POST \
  -H 'X-Sumo-Category:lab/aws/cloudtrail' -T replay.jsonl "$SUMO_URL"
```

== Exercise: this query returns nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
First set the time range to the lab's date only, leaving out step 10's replays. This query returns nothing. Why, and what is the quickest way to prove it rather than guess? "Show the answer" opens answers in the guide.
```sumo
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "sourceIpAddress" as ip
| count by ip
```

== Answer: nodrop proves the key is wrong
section: Hands-on exercises
Keys are case-sensitive and CloudTrail spells it sourceIPAddress, so the json operator drops all 24 events. With nodrop they survive, and this query returns one row, (missing) with 24: the scope found the events, and the key did not.
```sumo
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "sourceIpAddress" as ip nodrop
| if(isBlank(ip), "(missing)", ip) as ip
| count by ip
```

== Exercise: a faster D2 that returns nothing
section: Hands-on exercises
A colleague speeds up this fixed D2 by adding the keyword AccessDenied to its scope, after _sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail, and leaves the rest alone. On the lab's date only, it returns nothing again. Explain, and suggest a keyword that is safe.
```sumo
// D2: every denial code, per source address, 5 or more
_sourceCategory=lab/aws/cloudtrail
| json "errorCode", "sourceIPAddress" nodrop
| where errorCode in ("AccessDenied", "AccessDeniedException", "Client.UnauthorizedOperation", "UnauthorizedOperation")
| count by sourceIPAddress
| where _count >= 5
```

== Answer: keywords filter before the first pipe
section: Hands-on exercises
The three EC2 denials contain Client.UnauthorizedOperation, never the word AccessDenied, so they are gone before the where operator sees them, and 203.0.113.50 is back to 4. A safe keyword is errorCode: only events with an error contain that word.

== Common mistakes
section: Common mistakes
- A dropped or mis-cased key, a narrow scope or the default 15-minute range returns nothing, like a quiet account.
- Keywords ignore case; json keys and the where operator's string comparisons do not.
- An upload's 200 means received, not stored.
